    <GaugingStation location="Invertruim" name="Spey" number="8007">
        <Comments>
            <Comment type="Station Summary">Velocity area station. The highest station on the Spey.</Comment>
            <Comment type="Hydrometric">Natural control; frequent rating changes. Bypassing at flows above 1.8m on both banks. Rating derived from current meter gaugings up to 2.589m/195.2 cumecs measured. The record between 1995 and 2000 is either of poor quality due to broken inlet pipes or is missing. A station rebuild was completed on 09/05/2000 resulting in improved data quality thereafter. One peak flow rating across period of record.</Comment>
            <Comment type="General">Velocity area station, 45m wide Cableway rated. Highest station on the Spey. Station re-built in May 2000.</Comment>
            <Comment type="Flow record">Unrepresentative period from 01/01/1995 to 28/10/1998 as inlet pipes broken resulted in a deteriorating data quality, then missing period caused by further deterioration of data quality (28/10/1998 and 24/02/2000) and station rebuild (24/02/2000 to 09/05/2000). Full period of record peak flow data reviewed and released in April 2017 (WINFAP Files v5) and then revised again in August 2024 (WINFAP Files v13).</Comment>
            <Comment type="Flow regime">200.4 sq.km controlled for hydro-electric power by British Aluminium, 86.4 sq.km controlled by Scottish Electric (total 72%); diversions and storage influence regime, major reduction in runoff. Reservoirs for hydropower: Spey Dam (1944), Cuaich Dam (1961).</Comment>
            <Comment type="Catchment">Mountainous headwaters, often snowy in winter. Granite and Moinian metamorphic. Mainly moorland, limited pastoral in valley bottoms. Cuaich Dam impounded during period of record (1961).</Comment>
            <Comment type="Factors affecting runoff">H</Comment>
        </Comments>
